        {
            "title": "The Joy Of Destruction In Silent Storm",
            "date": "2016-02-23 14:00:56",
            "url": "https://steamstore-a.akamaihd.net/news/externalpost/rps/295354025642901993",
            "content": "<p><img src=\"https://www.rockpapershotgun.com/images/16/feb/silentstormheader.jpg\" alt=\"\" /></p>\n<p>It&#8217;s a strange time to look at old turn-based shooters. While we&#8217;re hardly swamped, the genre has left its dormancy, casting strange shadows on our old sacred cows.  Whisper it now: maybe some of those old things we championed for so long aren&#8217;t all that great?</p>\n<p>Fortunately, <a href=\"https://www.rockpapershotgun.com/tag/silent-storm/\">Silent Storm</a> can ignore such insinuations, as it was never sacred. Its 2003 release by Nival Interactive caused little splash, and it didn&#8217;t find the legendary status of UFO or the dedicated mod scene of Jagged Alliance 2.</p>\n<p>Playing it now, that strikes me as completely unfair.</p>\n<p> &#8230; [<a href=\"https://www.rockpapershotgun.com/2016/02/23/silent-storm-retrospective/\">visit site to read more</a>]</p>\n"
        },
